import fs from 'node:fs/promises';
const read = async name => JSON.parse(await fs.readFile(new URL(`../../app/data/${name}`, import.meta.url)));
const evidence = await read('cache-selector-evidence.2026-10-07.v1.json');
const policy = await read('cache-component-policy.2026-10-07.v1.json');
const original = evidence.records.filter(r => r.cohort === 'original-unresolved');
const accepted = original.filter(r => r.components.length);
const unresolved = original.filter(r => !r.components.length);
const esc = value => String(value ?? '').replaceAll('|', '/').replaceAll('\n', ' ');
const table = (head, rows) => `\n| ${head.join(' | ')} |\n| ${head.map(() => '---').join(' | ')} |\n${rows.map(row => `| ${row.map(esc).join(' | ')} |`).join('\n')}\n`;
let text = `# Retained selector spatial correspondence\n
The complete original cohort of **${original.length} unresolved selectors** was checked against the audited copied-cache locations and exact closed-scenario tile components. **${accepted.length} additional selectors** have defensible correspondence; **${unresolved.length} remain unresolved**. The runtime policy now maps **${policy.mapping.filter(r => r.components.length).length} of ${policy.mapping.length} selectors**, with **${policy.mapping.filter(r => r.project).length}** allowing automatic projection. There are **${policy.mapping.filter(r => !r.components.length && r.legacy.endsWith('-0')).length}** unresolved unsplit primaries. Physical walking evidence and resource correspondence remain separate.\n
The [complete evidence file](../../app/data/cache-selector-evidence.2026-10-07.v1.json) includes all 453 original selectors plus five previously mapped priority reviews. Each row retains the Object, NPC, Monster, Spawn and Connect declarations, exact sibling-exclusive object names, all matched copied placements, original source coordinates/archive references, rotated decoded rectangle, complete cardinal perimeter witnesses, and accepted or unresolved reason. Every accepted runtime mapping includes its witness coordinates and original unresolved boundary/signature provenance in [the generated policy](../../app/data/cache-component-policy.2026-10-07.v1.json). Existing 609 physical correspondences keep their original methods and components. New correspondence does not certify that every retained resource remains present or interactable.\n
## Evidence and acceptance rule\n
Sources are the audit's full <code>walkability.v1.json.gz</code> component masks and the read-only <code>rs3-cache-work/cache-copy</code> location, object-definition and layout databases. The generator verifies copied js5-5, js5-16 and js5-23 SHA-256 values against the audited manifest, and checks decoder interpreter/constants hashes. Those hashes and the full walkability hash are saved with the evidence. Original game files and private saves are not opened.\n
A retained Object name must match a decoded placement name exactly and occur in only this section among its siblings. No stripping qualifiers, approximate names or guessed quantities selects a placement. Unsplit -0 selectors use their top-level declarations. Only original plane-0 locations are used; conditional morph names cannot certify a correspondence. All matching placements in the declared region are retained, including disagreements. NPCs, monster spawns and ground-item spawns have no authoritative server placement catalogue in this copied export; their declarations are recorded but cannot manufacture coordinates.\n
A walkable placement tile identifies its exact component. Otherwise, the complete cardinal perimeter of the decoded, rotated rectangular footprint supplies possible spatial witnesses, restricted to this region and the placement's actual source floor; an object below a rendered bridge cannot identify the overlying bridge component. The perimeter does not prove an interaction or an open door. Every usable unconditional exclusive anchor must have the same unique common component. One ambiguous footprint stays unresolved; disjoint anchors stay unresolved; a second anchor can disambiguate a perimeter only when the common component actually touches both. There is no fragment-size ranking, nearest-tile search, majority vote, candidate ordering, inferred bridge opening or arbitrary radius. Anchors with no walkable witness are recorded, never moved.\n
This deliberately does not union disjoint resource fragments into an invented single walking section. A section whose exclusive declarations span several components needs reviewed resource geometry or separate source attribution. Existing walking mappings with conflicting object witnesses are retained for traversal but marked <code>resourceUnresolved</code>. Every one of the 1,062 retained selectors is also audited for unlocated declared contents: any NPC, Monster, Shop, Spawn or Connect lacking exact placement evidence, or any Object name not completely corroborated to its mapped component, marks resource correspondence unresolved. Raw numeric location keys and keys matching named/numeric retained location definitions are also unlocated entrance/area aliases, even when they occur outside Connect; for example 12342-3 declares 12610 directly. The generated policy records these under <code>LocationAlias</code>, lists exact unlocated declarations per row, and uses reason <code>unlocated-declared-contents-or-entrances</code>. There are **${policy.mapping.filter(r => r.resourceUnresolved).length} resource-uncertain selectors**. Unresolved selectors and shared component aliases receive the same resource flag. This also applies to boundary-signature mappings: a walking correspondence cannot locate all contents or entrances. Consumers must keep such retained resource access separate from component seeding.\n
## Priority and named source findings\n
Task Chunks/Sections declarations also require a task-location witness even if the retained section has no listed objects or NPCs. The audit expands only declared chunksPlus aliases (with cycle protection and unambiguous alias names); a bare numeric base reference marks every retained selector of that base as potentially used. Every affected row stores <code>declaredTaskLocations</code> with permanent task ID, category, raw label, field, original token and expanded location, and a <code>TaskLocation</code> unlocated declaration. No task location is inferred from a nearby mapped object. In particular, **12348-2** retains Filthy Rich's declared action location and **13107-4** retains the Lumbridge cow-pen compass clue location as resource-uncertain, despite empty section contents.\n
* **13361-1, Kharid-et:** existing component 1 is independently corroborated by the declared workbench at (3343,3197) and bank chest at (3356,3198). Their full footprint evidence is in the priority row.\n
* **13620-2, Nature grotto:** newly mapped to component 15. All three retained Stones match placements at (3439,3336), (3440,3335), and (3441,3336); every witness agrees. Resource correspondence remains unresolved: these Stones do not certify the Connect 9043 interior entrance. Filliman Tarlock occurs in both section 1 and section 2, so his name alone cannot disambiguate them. Washing bowl is a ground spawn without copied server coordinates.\n
* **13620-3, Polypore entrance:** still unresolved. Its sole declaration is Connect 18517 (Polypore Dungeon). The copied region has Cave entrance 63093 at (3410,3330), footprint 2×2, whose only in-region perimeter witnesses are component 4 at (3410,3329)/(3411,3329). Neither the retained declaration nor the static object definition uniquely certifies that this cave is the declared Polypore entrance, so the plausible component is not accepted.\n
* **13618-1, Abandoned Mine:** existing walking component 10 is retained, with resource uncertainty. Cart tunnel at (3430,3233) witnesses 10; (3429,3225) touches 10/11; (3440,3232) touches 13/14. Mine carts also witness 14. The retained Mithril/Coal veins are in the separate named Abandoned Mine interior, not surface coordinates certified by this export. These discrepancies do not prove the veins or tunnels inaccessible in game.\n
* **12092-5, Lava Maze:** existing component 8 is shared with section 4, so automatic resource projection remains false and resource correspondence unresolved. The only retained section-5 anchor is Mysterious ghost plus the named dungeon connection; neither has certified coordinates here. A copied Ladder at (3017,3849) touches component 2, but its generic name does not identify which retained dungeon connection it serves. Fishing spot (lava) is retained in other selectors/interiors; the copied static-location export does not certify its server fishing-spawn placement.\n
* **13108-2, Guidor:** existing component 2 remains a walking mapping with resource uncertainty. Retained Bookcase/Drawers anchors witness 2/5/6, and one drawer at (3321,3385) witnesses 11. Guidor and his wife have no certified copied server placements. The static section mapping cannot place either NPC on a specific component.\n
* **Philipe Carnillean:** his retained locations include conditional quest-point locations and upper floors/interiors. No NPC spawn coordinate is inferred from a same-region object. For example, 9273-1 has no sibling-exclusive coordinate anchor; Mountain Camp 11065-1 and Shilo 11310-1 have conflicting object witnesses. These remain unresolved. Already mapped selectors keep their provenance; a surface component does not remove their retained NPC/task requirements.\n
## Reproduction\n
Run from this checkout; the three commands write only authored evidence, policy and this report:\n
\`\`\`powershell
node scripts/cache/audit-selector-mapping.mjs C:/Users/User/Projects/rs3-cache-work
node scripts/cache/build-runtime-topology.mjs C:/Users/User/Projects/rs3-cache-work/outputs/cache-audit-2026-10-07-v4/membership.v1.json
node scripts/cache/report-selector-mapping.mjs
node --test tests/cache-selector-mapping.test.mjs tests/cache-topology-application.test.mjs tests/cache-task-access.test.mjs
\`\`\`\n
The audit cohort is identified by original unresolved provenance, so rerunning after acceptance still examines exactly the original 453. The tests cover exact versus perimeter witnesses, complete-perimeter ambiguity, agreement versus conflicting anchors, conditional and missing coverage, foreign component rejection, original provenance retention, every accepted evidence row and priority resource flags.\n
## All accepted additions\n`;
text += table(['Retained selector', 'Name', 'Cache component', 'Matched exclusive object names'], accepted.map(r => [r.legacy, r.name, r.components.join(', '), r.foundNames.join(', ')]));
text += '\n## Unresolved reason totals\n';
text += table(['Reason', 'Selectors'], [...new Set(unresolved.map(r => r.reason))].sort().map(reason => [reason, unresolved.filter(r => r.reason === reason).length]));
text += '\n## Complete unresolved remainder\n\nEvery unresolved selector is listed below. Empty/no-exclusive anchors include empty sections, names shared among siblings, unmatched exact names, named connections lacking an identified entrance, and declarations whose NPC/monster/item placements are outside this static export. The evidence JSON distinguishes these with the complete declaration and matched-name inventory. Conflicts retain all spatial witnesses for further review.\n';
text += table(['Retained selector', 'Name', 'Reason', 'Matched exclusive names / coordinate anchors'], unresolved.map(r => [r.legacy, r.name, r.reason, r.foundNames.join(', ') || 'none']));
await fs.writeFile(new URL('../../docs/research/cache-selector-mapping.md', import.meta.url), text);
console.log(`Wrote mapping report: ${accepted.length} additions, ${unresolved.length} unresolved selectors.`);
